WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.830
In the last few years, drill rap has gone
viral…

00:00:07.695 --> 00:00:11.760
It made headlines in 2022, when the New York
City Mayor Eric Adams said he wanted to ban

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.330
drill music videos.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:17.080
And the rocketing success of artists like
Lil Durk and the late Pop Smoke has pushed

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.529
the drill style to the top of the charts.

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:23.660
But what makes drill so interesting is not
just the music itself, but also the culture

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.820
and lifestyle that surrounds it.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.410
With its gritty depictions of street life
and its uncompromising attitude, drill has

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:39.590
become a voice for people in urban areas.

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:44.250
This story, is about how a genre from the
streets of Chicago ended up influencing the

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.726
music of major cities all around the globe.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:59.410
Originating in Chicago over a decade ago,
the sound of drill music has taken over other

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:04.400
major cities like London, New York, and Sydney.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.930
Chicago has a long history as a musical city
and has produced legendary rappers like Common,

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:14.180
Lupe Fiasco, Kanye West, Da Brat, and more.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:18.909
But during the 2010s the city became known
for a new style of Hip Hop that was heavier

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:23.700
and darker, and sounded closer to the trap
music being made in Atlanta.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.659
The word drill itself is slang and has a wide
range of meanings, but is often associated

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:31.600
with shooting, violence and robbing.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.829
I sat down with Mark Braboy a music journalist
from Chicago that covers drill music.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:40.759
How would you describe the word drill as Chicago
slang?

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:44.860
Drill by the most part usually carries a double
meaning..

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.620
like it’s a big party, it’s a big function,
it’s a big event.. lets go to this big thing

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:56.030
that is happening or a euphemism for lets
do a hit let's do a drive by shooting

00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:03.909
In 2010 it became associated with music, when
the late rapper PacMan released “It’s

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:10.330
A Drill.”

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.320
One of PacMan’s friends King Louie made
his come-up by handing out his mixtapes at

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.100
bus stops and parties.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:20.750
King Louie described drill as a term for anything,
saying it’s “a way of life…

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.540
It’s a verb.”

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:27.269
Drill music wasn’t born out of any specific
production styles or sounds, but was an entire

00:02:27.269 --> 00:02:31.640
culture that represented the lifestyle of
the people in Chicago’s southside.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.550
What do you think people commonly misunderstand
about drill?

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.720
One of the biggest ones is that it’s entirely
about violence.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.720
These rappers are now processing the trauma
of the grief that they’ve experienced throughout

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:46.900
their lives.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.959
As a Chicago rapper Noname once said, “it’s
real-life therapy sessions.”

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:56.140
Starting in 2011, Chicago drill rappers began
promoting their music with music videos on

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:01.040
YouTube, and that’s when the music caught
the attention of those outside of Chicago.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.940
YouTube helped jumpstart the careers of Chicago
rappers Lil Durk, G Herbo, and more, as they

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.989
uploaded songs that depicted the truth of
gang life and the issues that plagued their

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.040
environment.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.041
But there was one rapper who would serve as
the spark and help introduce drill culture

00:03:15.041 --> 00:03:16.041
to the world.

00:03:16.041 --> 00:03:21.739
In 2011, a 16-year-old by the name of Chief
Keef began uploading music videos filmed at

00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:27.960
his grandma’s house while under house arrest.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:36.370
His song I Don’t Like, became an anthem
and even caught the attention of Kanye West

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.739
who remixed the song with Pusha T, Jadakiss,
and Big Sean.

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:46.430
Chief Keef catapulted into popularity and
would go on to drop Love Sosa which has pulled

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:49.939
in over 270 millions views.

00:03:49.939 --> 00:03:53.739
But it was Young Chop’s production that
would form the influential sound of Chicago

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:55.260
drill music.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.040
Sharing a lot of similarities with Atlanta’s
trap music, Young Chop combined dark piano

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:13.750
melodies with heavy 808s, busy hi-hats, and
halftime drums with the snare on the 3.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.040
You can hear similar elements in Lex Luger’s
production on Hard In Da Paint, especially

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:23.449
the busy hi-hats and snare on the 3.

00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:33.180
But Young Chop said drill music is darker,
adding “we brought the darker keys… deeper,

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.740
scarier, hit harder.”

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.350
Following the viral success of Chief Keef,
UK rappers took notice and began creating

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:42.120
their own music inspired by Chicago drill.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.889
Before drill, grime music was popular in the
UK during the early 2000s.

00:04:46.889 --> 00:04:51.789
Grime is a style of dance music similar to
Hip Hop that pulls more from Jamaican dancehall

00:04:51.789 --> 00:04:59.330
and UK garage.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:04.470
But by the 2010’s grime’s growing popularity
began to get stagnant, making room for UK

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.470
drill.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:10.169
UK drill formed in South London around 2013
and combined Chicago’s drill style with

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:14.420
elements from grime and road rap (another
UK style of rap).

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:24.580
You can hear production similar to Young Chop’s
in UK rap songs like “Lets Get It” By

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:27.550
Stickz.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:32.150
But many point to “Kennington Where It Started”
from 2017 as the song that put the unique

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:38.730
UK sound on the map.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:43.470
The song discusses gang violence with a UK
style flow similar to road rap and grime before

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:44.470
it.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.639
The beat itself is faster than a Chicago drill
beat.

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:51.420
It infuses drill with elements of grime, using
more slices and drop outs to make the hits

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.789
harder.

00:05:54.789 --> 00:06:03.350
UK Drill gained attention worldwide when comedian
Michael Dapaah released the viral song “Man’s

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:04.350
Not Hot.”

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:10.389
The track jokes around with the UK drill style
and today has over 400 million views on YouTube.

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:15.849
UK drill has continued to grow in popularity
and in 2021 Russ Millions and Tion Wayne dropped

00:06:15.849 --> 00:06:23.900
Body, which became the first drill song to
reach #1 on the UK charts.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:29.110
As drill has continued to make waves in the
UK the sound has made its way across the pond

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.720
and influenced the drill music of New York
City.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:35.270
But long before the UK drill sound made its
way to New York…

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:47.430
a rapper named Bobby Shmurda put Brooklyn
on the drill map with his song from 2014.

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:52.199
In the track Shmurda raps about street crime
and his life growing up in Brooklyn, but the

00:06:52.199 --> 00:06:55.110
beat is distinctly unique from Chicago drill
rap.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:59.740
More sparse and less dark, producer Jahlil
Beats says he was inspired by Bronx producer

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:01.370
Swizz Beatz.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:18.740
Specifically “his drum patterns and his
double snare.”

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:24.610
But it was 22Gz and Sheff G that helped expand
Brooklyn drill and they incorporated the UK

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:28.580
sound by working with UK producers like AXL
Beats and 808 Melo.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:37.610
You can hear the UK beats in 22Gz 2016 single
Suburban produced by AXL Beats.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:42.319
The drill sound spread to the other boroughs
of New York too.

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:48.900
Like the Bronx, where Cardi B made her come
up with the song Red Barz.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:57.140
I talked to DMunna 1Hunna an up and coming
rapper from the Bronx to learn more about

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:58.730
New York drill.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.080
How did you get introduced to drill music?

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.650
To be honest, when I was young, I first got
started listening to drill music by listen

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:11.150
to Chicago artists Chief Keef, Lil Durk and
G Herbo when they first started coming up

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:12.150
so.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:18.360
Those were one of the first artists I was
listening to of drill music and then started

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.800
switching to New York once I got older and
stuff.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.879
What sets New York drill apart from the drill
in other cities?

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:25.680
Well, I love what we do in the Bronx..

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:27.169
We just we got a different energy.

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:33.339
We just brought a whole different energy like
Brooklyn probably came before us but like

00:08:33.339 --> 00:08:38.890
Bronx drill really woke a lot of people up
like may it made everybody want to be a drill

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:39.890
rapper like.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:42.729
It was like another Chicago thing again.

00:08:42.729 --> 00:08:48.120
We brought it back again, that different energy.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:55.830
Then in 2019, everything changed when Pop
Smoke released his debut mixtape Meet the

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:56.830
Woo.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:09:01.490
Pop Smoke collaborated with London producer
808Melo and infused his music with elements

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:06.860
from Brooklyn, UK drill, and grime.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:14.320
The two lead singles “Dior,” and “Welcome
to the Party” went platinum in the U.S.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.070
and made Pop Smoke a rising star in the international
drill scene.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:24.670
In February 2020, Pop Smoke was shot and killed
during a home invasion at a house he was renting

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:25.709
in L.A.

00:09:25.709 --> 00:09:30.560
The rising star died before releasing his
first studio album and his upcoming tour was

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:31.620
canceled.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.840
Pop Smoke is one of many rappers to have been
killed in recent years due to gun violence.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:40.600
The true threat of violence that some face
is shown to us with each new headline of another

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:41.710
rapper killed.

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:45.540
The violence depicted within the music and
the violence surrounding the music has given

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.730
drill a negative connotation to some.

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:51.320
Drill artists have been pulled from festival
lineups, and have had their music removed

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:52.690
from Instagram.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:59.790
In 2019, Youtube took down 102 UK drill music
videos after a request from the British police.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:05.440
In 2018, members of the UK rap group CGM were
given a court order that banned them from

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:07.240
making music without police permission.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:13.000
And though Chief Keef is one of the most influential
drill figures, he can’t return to his hometown

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:18.380
of Chicago, reportedly for outstanding warrants
and because the former mayor has effectively

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:19.450
banned him.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:24.370
All of this has left us with the age-old question:
does music reflect your environment or shape

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:25.380
it?

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:30.010
For drill music, it is a reflection of the
realities that young people in low-income

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:31.010
areas face.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:35.250
“When you talk about places like New York
and things of that nature, and Chicago, to

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:41.209
say that it’s solely or heavily responsible
for a lot of that violence is a wild over-exaggeration.

00:10:41.209 --> 00:10:47.920
The reality of where this music comes from,
this music comes from extreme poverty.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:53.040
The music comes from violence that has gotten
out of control that the young teenagers were

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:54.170
born into.”

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:58.430
What do people commonly misunderstand about
drill?

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:04.680
I feel like they feel like everything's all
negative and stuff like that.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:07.690
So it's like some stuff could be negative.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:11.100
Maybe it's just some stuff that we’ve seen,
been through and maybe people that’s their

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:13.370
past they trying to talk about what they been
through.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:17.510
Sometimes it's stuff where it's not even negative
its just a party song, but it got a drill

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:21.840
beat to it

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.769
Because of drill music’s heavy online presence,
its influence is continuing to grow and spread

00:11:25.769 --> 00:11:27.780
to more cities around the world.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:32.970
Today you can find drill scenes in Australia,
Finland, and South Korea.

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:42.830
Drill has even fused with latin genres like
reggaeton to create latin drill.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:46.510
And drill’s influence continues to evolve
with newcomers to the scene putting their

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:48.370
own spin on the genre.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:53.330
Bronx rapper Ice Spice has blown up with her
calm, attitude driven style.

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:58.120
Known for its harsh, dark sound, Ice Spice
expands the sound of drill by using pop elements

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:09.310
to make her music up-tempo while bringing
femininity to the genre.

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:13.380
With all of the evolution that drill music
has seen in its history, it’s hard to pinpoint

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.170
what exactly is drill.

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.699
Is it based on a region?

00:12:16.699 --> 00:12:18.420
A sound?

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:19.620
Lyrical content?

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.600
Or maybe King Louie was right, it’s none
of that.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:43.149
It’s “a way of life… a verb.”

